  "reason": "Evidence: The best-performing configuration is config_015 (multiprofile, scvi_n200, resnet50, transformer, weighted) at 0.7131 acc. All high-performing runs use resnet50 as the image encoder, yet image-only performance is very low (0.2079), suggesting the image modality's contribution may be underutilized. The image encoder is the one major design axis not yet varied in the multiprofile multimodal regime. Question: Does upgrading the image encoder from resnet50 to imagenet_vit_l (a stronger, larger pretrained backbone) improve the best-known configuration? Why this is the best next experiment: config_001 changes exactly one variable (image_encoder) from the current best (config_015), keeping scvi_n200, transformer aggregation, weighted fusion, and multiprofile fixed. This cleanly isolates the image encoder effect. If it improves performance, it identifies the image modality as a bottleneck worth further investment; if it does not, it confirms the current image encoder is sufficient and redirects attention to other axes (e.g., RNA encoder or aggregation). This is a high-value exploitation step that tests a logically motivated extension of the strongest evidence without conflating multiple changes."
